      "math_academy_id": "q-41944",
      "sequence_position": 69,
      "problem": "Find the product $AB$, if\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]$\nand \n$B=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5} & 4 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\sqrt{2} & -3.2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}].$",
      "worked_solution": "Notice that $A$ is the $2$nd *row* of the identity matrix ${I}_{3}.$\n\nSince we're multiplying the $3\\times 3$ matrix $B$ by the $2$nd *row* of the identity matrix ${I}_{3}$ on the *left*, the result must be the $2$nd *row* of $B.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nAB=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2\\sqrt{2} & -3.2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nLet's check the long way:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5} & 4 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\sqrt{2} & -3.2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2\\sqrt{2} & -3.2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$",
